\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Component-wise ablation across three outcomes: (a) system latency (\% reduction), (b) simulated cognitive load (\% change), and (c) time-to-correct-information (\% change vs.\ TF-IDF control). JEPA prefetching drives the primary latency reduction ($-22.2\%$ to $-26.9\%$). Confidence scaling (Full GDT vs.\ CMA) contributes an additional 4.7\% latency reduction. GSI gate alone has negligible latency and cognitive load effects ($-0.50\%$ and $-0.30\%$), but actively triggers on 34.7\% of transitions. Time-to-information remains within ${\pm }0.22\%$ of control across all variants due to the 300-s censoring floor.}}{13}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ablation}{{2}{13}{Component-wise ablation across three outcomes: (a) system latency (\% reduction), (b) simulated cognitive load (\% change), and (c) time-to-correct-information (\% change vs.\ TF-IDF control). JEPA prefetching drives the primary latency reduction ($-22.2\%$ to $-26.9\%$). Confidence scaling (Full GDT vs.\ CMA) contributes an additional 4.7\% latency reduction. GSI gate alone has negligible latency and cognitive load effects ($-0.50\%$ and $-0.30\%$), but actively triggers on 34.7\% of transitions. Time-to-information remains within ${\pm }0.22\%$ of control across all variants due to the 300-s censoring floor}{figure.2}{}}
